            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> Volunteering in Our Community</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Simplify articles for easier understanding. Text: Paraphrase short passages in a simple fashion.</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can simplify articles on community support, using visuals or diagrams for main points (e.g., "This diagram shows how Jóvenes Unidos por la Educación provides support to improve school facilities, increase resources, and create safer learning environments.")<br>Can paraphrase short passages on volunteer work, using gestures and simple language (e.g., "Volunteering means helping without being paid, like when we clean the streets or help at food banks. I can show this by acting like I'm picking up trash and handing out food to people.")<br>Can explain complex community terms using simple definitions and examples (e.g., "Volunteering is when people help others without expecting money, like organizing a park clean-up or helping at an animal shelter. It's about supporting the community.")</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

## SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE
<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays 3-4 vivid images of local volunteer activities (e.g., park clean-up, food bank distribution, animal shelter work). Students, in pairs, briefly describe what they see and try to explain the purpose of each activity in simple terms. Then, the teacher asks: "What do these images have in common?" leading to the concept of 'volunteering'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher presents a complex sentence from a hypothetical article: "Governmental agencies have been continuously allocating substantial resources to underserved communities to promote sustainable development and improve public services." The teacher then models simplifying it: "The government has been giving money and help to poor areas to make them better and provide public services." The teacher highlights how content words are stressed (GOV-ern-ment, GIV-ing, MON-ey, HELP, POOR, A-reas, BET-ter, PUB-lic SERV-ic-es).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b><br>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I simplify a sentence, am I trying to make it more complex or easier to understand?" (Expected answer: "Easier to understand.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do I need to use the exact same words, or can I use my own simple vocabulary?" (Expected answer: "Your own simple words.")</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Supporting Our Community"</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short article excerpt (approx. 150 words) from a local NGO website about a recent "Volunteer Brigade" initiative aimed at improving school facilities in an underserved area. The text includes phrases like "Community programs are funded by the government," "Individuals should contribute to community projects," and "Volunteers have been working to improve community services," integrating passive voice, modals for necessity, and present perfect continuous, along with key vocabulary (e.g., <i>resources, development, impact, participants, supportive</i>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Engaging in discussions about civic responsibilities: "How has the local government been encouraging more people to volunteer in our community?" Explore how different communities express and value support needs through language and communication: "Why has community involvement been important for everyone?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b><br>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> "What is the main purpose of the 'Volunteer Brigade' described in the article?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match two complex statements from the article about funding or impact to their simplified definitions provided by the teacher.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>Accuracy focus on Mediation</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice simplifying articles and paraphrasing passages using passive voice ("Community programs are funded by the government."), modals for necessity ("Individuals should contribute to community projects."), and present perfect continuous ("Volunteers have been working to improve community services."). Vocabulary: <i>government, programs, resources, services, responsibilities, volunteers, projects, community, influence, public services, development, impact, initiative, shelter, underserved, meaningful, positive, crucial, responsible, active, impactful, essential, collaborate, clean up</i>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b><br>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Sentence Simplification:</b> The teacher provides 5-6 complex sentences related to community support (from the article or similar texts), each containing a target grammar structure (passive voice, modal, present perfect continuous) and 2-3 new vocabulary words. Students work individually to rewrite each sentence using simpler language, aiming for clarity. Example: "Sustained public involvement is crucial for the long-term development of community-based initiatives." Simplified: "People's active involvement is essential for local projects to grow."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Paraphrasing Pairs:</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher gives each pair a short, complex passage (2-3 sentences) describing a specific volunteer project (e.g., building a community shelter, organizing a "volunteer brigade" to "clean up" a park). Each pair must paraphrase the passage for an imagined younger audience or someone unfamiliar with the topic, using simple language and considering gestures. They practice reading their simplified versions aloud, focusing on clear pronunciation and stressing content words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Explain a Term, Peer Check:</b> In their pairs, students take turns explaining one complex community term (e.g., "underserved," "civic responsibility," "sustainable development," "governmental influence") to their partner using simple definitions and concrete examples. The partner listens and provides feedback on clarity and simplicity. The teacher circulates, listening for effective mediation and use of target vocabulary.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-17 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Supporting Our Community"</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4) to develop a short "pitch" (2-3 minutes) for their "Volunteer Recruitment Video" project. The focus for this mediation lesson is on *how they will simplify and explain* a complex aspect of volunteering or community need in their video to appeal to a broad audience. Each group must choose one specific volunteer initiative (e.g., supporting an animal shelter, improving local resources, participating in a clean-up project) and prepare to explain its importance, the responsibilities involved, and the positive impact, using simplified language, examples, and potentially gestures/diagram ideas for their video. They should integrate at least one instance of passive voice, one modal for necessity, and one present perfect continuous sentence in their explanation. For example, "Many community projects are funded by grants, but volunteers are crucial. Individuals should contribute their time because volunteers have been making a huge impact on local development for years."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Groups will present their "pitch" to the class, emphasizing how they'll make complex ideas accessible. This directly prepares them for recording their actual video, focusing on the mediation aspect of simplifying their message. They will collaboratively discuss diverse solutions for community involvement for their video message, practicing sociolinguistic competence.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>Formative assessment strategy</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on effective mediation (simplification, paraphrasing, clear explanations) and integration of target language.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group presents their "pitch" for their Volunteer Recruitment Video, specifically demonstrating how they will simplify a complex idea (e.g., explaining "civic responsibility" or the "impact of collaborative involvement" in simple terms, or showing how a diagram would simplify a point about "resource allocation").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b><br>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and simplicity of explanations (Mediation).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of paraphrasing and examples.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Integration of target vocabulary (e.g., <i>impact, resources, initiative, essential</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of passive voice, modals for necessity, and present perfect continuous.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation, with appropriate stress on content words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and engagement in the group presentation.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts:<br>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One complex idea about community support that I can now explain simply is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I found it easy/challenging to (simplify/paraphrase) because..."</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-8 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b><br>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How has practicing mediation skills helped me think about communicating effectively in my volunteer recruitment video?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of simplifying information for a diverse audience do I still need to improve?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The focus on simplifying information for the video project was highly engaging for students, as it provided a clear, practical application for mediation skills. The modeling of stress patterns helped improve fluency and intelligibility. Some groups found it challenging to consistently use the present perfect continuous while simplifying; adjustments for future lessons could include more targeted practice on this tense in sentence rewriting exercises. Scaffolding for the next theme will involve re-emphasizing the importance of clear communication and information transfer, regardless of the topic.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes our theme on "Volunteering in Our Community," with students successfully practicing mediation skills for their project. The ability to simplify and explain complex ideas will be an essential tool in future themes where understanding and conveying information effectively is crucial.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>